many european countries were some of the  worst hit by covid19 when it first swept  the world  last year the world health organization  says europe is now the epicenter  spain now has more deaths than china  europe's response to the pandemic and  the vaccine rollout  has been criticized for being  disorganized and slow  but would the outcome have been  different if europe had more female  leaders  i think you know out of the attributes  associated with leadership  to deal with the pandemic on 13 out of  19  women actually come ahead of men  christine lagarde president of the  european central bank  spoke to zany minton bedos the economist  editor-in-chief  about the long-term damage of covert 19  in europe  what do you see as the longer term  scarring consequences of this and i'm  thinking for example of a of  something that i know you have been very  concerned about long before the pandemic  which is the  widening of inequality that we've seen  around the world it's something you  raised the alarm on  when you were at the imf very very  effectively in so many ways this  pandemic has not just  exposed existing inequalities it's  worsened them um  how much does that worry you and and  frankly what do we need to do about it  i think it raises the question of what  kind of economy are we going to  um land in i don't want to say go back  to because i think that  the economy that we will land in uh  are that will we will that will have  morphed into  when the pandemic is always going to be  different from what what we've  experienced  pre-pandemic so i think the way in which  we we  we buy we pay uh we learn we  look after our health will be different  and probably uh probably for good so  that's one of the uh  the good outcome if you will of the  transformation that we are seeing now if  you look at  the not so positive outcome clearly  there has been  a worsening of inequalities and  while this is not for a central bank to  actually address  and try to find the policies that will  respond to the inequalities  i think it will be upon governments to  identify the set of polities  policies that will best respond to  closing  reducing addressing those inequalities  it won't surprise you but i will mention  women  as those that have actually heavily  suffered from  the worsened inequalities  caused by the pandemic particularly  because of the uh  you know the gray the grey work or the  uh the informal  work which is predominantly done by  women and which are the first  uh to go in in situations that we're  going through at the moment  but if you look at the proportion of  women that are a  victim of the current um  furlough schemes often followed by  layoff  women are taking a much heavier burden  than men  it's it's really documented now very  clearly  if you look at the proportion of men and  women who are working  in those industries particularly the  service industries that are subject to  social  distancing and as a result are being hit  first predominantly women  you know you look at hospitality you  look at all those things  and if you look at those that are most  exposed to the pandemic  at the lower end of the scale in the  health sector in particular  you have many more women than men there  is a there's a kind of  i think interesting debate going on  right now and that countries which had  female leaders have done better in the  pandemic  um now we can discuss whether that to  what degree is true but certainly there  is  an argument that many are making that  that's true and you famously said if it  had been lehman sisters rather than  lehman brothers the world look would  look very different after the financial  crisis  you know is there something um about  female leadership that is different  would we have been better off if more  countries have been led by women during  this pandemic  there's been a harvard study that was  just recently published  it's an analysis of the perception that  people have had  of the way in which the crisis was  managed and there is an overwhelming  response  that shows again its perception that  people have had  that shows that they feel that women had  had a better way  to deal with the pandemic i think you  know out of the 19  attributes associated with leadership to  deal with the pandemic  on 13 out of 19 women actually come  ahead of men now is that to say that  they have done a fabulous job probably  not  uh is it to say that they had attributes  such as empathy such as appreciating the  fear that people had  such as reaching out that that was um  you know dominant in their way of  dealing with the crisis  probably so there are so fewer women  than men  actually in charge of those issues that  the fact that  many of them have been successful is  also an indication that  they're pretty damn good so what do you  then do to get more women  into those positions to have more women  in leadership organizing  you know pipes into which women are  supported or trained  or experienced are given role models  uh during that period of time when you  combine  learning jobs and and pushing yourself  as well as having children significant  you know on on-site support and and  you know financed  support made available and at the end of  the day  honestly targets or quotas depending on  how you know  what is the level of tolerance of the  organizations or the institutions  but simply saying okay if i have the  choice between  at the same quality level and training  between a man and a woman  if i have 20 women and 80 man i will  pick the woman  and i look at back 10 years ago when i  was  i was finance minister there was a law  that was pushed in french parliament  that required that  ultimately 40 of board members in all  french public companies should be women  10 years later they just celebrated the  anniversary of this law  it is now the case 40 of public  companies in france board members are  female so to anybody who says oh  terribly sorry they are not enough  competent female  oh not true if you have to find them you  find them europe is going to have  you know much less fiscal stimulus and i  think you've actually spoken about that  in the past  than than might have been anticipated so  you know isn't it disappointing in in  the recent past what has happened and  perhaps more importantly what will the  knock-on effect  of that be on the european economic  outlook it looks now as though europe's  going to have a slower weaker recovery  certainly than the one anticipated in  america and the fiscal response was  weaker in europe  than it was in in america that's for  sure the automatic stabilizers in europe  are  much larger stronger and provide better  welfare and coverage than what exists in  most american  states because a lot of those welfare  benefits are actually handled at state  levels  um so you we're really talking about  different economies but it is a fact  that um both member states and  and europe at large should continue to  provide  fiscal support uh to our economies  uh at least throughout 2021 and probably  beyond depending on  you know how the pandemic affects our  economy how the vaccination  uh process is rolled out how quickly  herd immunity is obtained  and how the race against the variance of  the virus  can be won by additional vaccinations or  by  you know additional therapies and how  our economies  actually transform now what is coming uh  in 21 is what was agreed  by the europeans and which is called  this pan-european next-generation eu  special funds hopefully will be spent  uh in the second half of 21 for the  first tranches that are due to  uh to to kick-start uh the investment  the focus on green the focus on digital  we have have gone digital the way we  live the way we work the way we shop the  way you and i are now communicating  has completely changed and i guess then  what is the consequence of that  for central banks has the pandemic shown  the world that it is time to make a  major switch  to digital trade and e-commerce and what  do you think the  banking landscape in particular looks  like in a digital age so  you know how how fast are you going to  change and to be very specific you know  when are you going to introduce a  digital currency at the ecb  i think more and more are convinced that  this is the way to go we should have a  digital currency which will not exclude  banknotes  we should have a digital currency that  is respectful  of privacy which is not selling data  which is not exploiting the data of  people that are using it  a digital currency that also uses the  canal of banks  because banks will be part and parcel in  that digital currency  uh launch and and then we will have to  you know refine it to the point that it  is completely safe  secure and can be used effectively with  less cost  more security as i said  respect for privacy of those that are  using it and on the voluntary basis  it's not to substitute existing  uh payment means so those who like  banknotes  um will continue to use their banknotes  but  those who want to use digital payments  should be able to do so  it's hard to say but i would say in the  next four years we should we should we  should be there  thank you christine lagarde lovely to  speak with you  i'm miranda johnson deputy executive  editor at the economist  if you'd like to read more of our  coverage on the coronavirus click on the  link  you can also listen to our new podcast  series about the global  vaccine rollout it's called the jab and  you can find it wherever you get your  podcasts  thanks for watching  you
 

